{"kind":"report","text":"ready-for-review at exact clean head b0947a298494b47fe8b85ae3e3dd50756ab149be, artifact 208dfcbd4deca48158ca6d10edd1e576e0cc484d.\n\nAll four of codex's blockers are closed.\n\nThe one status became two. A third party retiring the request ends the work; a third party retiring only the promise leaves the request live and able to take a replacement promise. Filing both as one terminal status made the second disappear from the bounded lists, which is quieter than the reneged row it replaced. Now revoked and released, neither terminal. I had named this exact risk when I asked for the review and shipped it anyway; the argument for why it mattered was codex's, not mine.\n\nThe fold profile advanced to workroom-fold@3, pinned by a test that fails if the fold's output moves again and the marker does not. A resident holding a checkpoint written by the old fold would otherwise have reused it under the new executable, making the projection depend on which checkpoints that process happened to hold.\n\nThe canonical documentation now describes the fold it has. work-loop.md carries ten statuses, both diagrams, who causes each, and why satisfaction is terminal while released is not. Both status reference pages name the new lanes.\n\nThe served bundle is regenerated and make ui-check passes, so the resident will not serve UI with no word for either status.\n\nThe simplification is taken: retiredByAnother returns a boolean per event rather than actor slices, since the target's author is known where the comparison happens.\n\nTwo things worth stating that are not blockers.\n\nThe docset surface gate caught me omitting the new value from the MCP page — the same gate, on the same class of omission, one cycle after it caught the previous one. Fixing that led me to docs/reference/mcp/status.md, which had the same gap and has no gate at all. I found it only by sweeping every file that mentions a status word. I have recorded the measurement against request 1f859eff, which already asks for one owner of the status vocabulary: adding a status touches eleven sites, and a gate covering one of them tells you that you are finished when you are not.\n\nAnd I refiled the artifact once, because the first named this head with an eight-character commit rather than the full forty. Nothing downstream resolves abbreviations — the connector's own proposal check compares that field character for character — so it was an identifier that only looked like one.\n\ngo vet, the full Go suite, tsc, the browser tests and ui-check all pass, and the worktree is clean at this head.\n\nOne constraint on merging, from codex and from hugh's standing direction: this must land after the resident-rebuild progress work, so a fold profile bump arrives together with honest rebuild reporting. That head is still awaiting its own review, so this one cannot merge yet whatever verdict it receives.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:208dfcbd4deca48158ca6d10edd1e576e0cc484d","branch":"request/commitment-honesty","head":"b0947a298494b47fe8b85ae3e3dd50756ab149be","status":"ready-for-review"}}